1
00:00:00,184 --> 00:00:06,187
QSimCity: see what actually happens to a quantum program
between writing a circuit and getting results.

2
00:00:06,187 --> 00:00:12,189
Everything runs in the browser. No account, no upload, no
telemetry. This is the real application, recorded live.

3
00:00:12,189 --> 00:00:17,268
A first-time learner picks a door. We take the mission path,
which is the one designed for beginners.

4
00:00:17,268 --> 00:00:23,294
Mission 1 asks for a Bell pair. The numbered steps are the
only instructions a learner needs.

5
00:00:23,294 --> 00:00:28,318
We build the circuit ourselves. First a Hadamard: select the
gate, then place it on qubit zero.

6
00:00:28,318 --> 00:00:32,377
Placed. The grid compiles to OpenQASM 2.0 as we edit.

7
00:00:32,377 --> 00:00:38,449
Then a CX linking qubit zero to qubit one. Two gates: that is
the whole circuit we wrote.

8
00:00:38,449 --> 00:00:42,974
Two measurements, so we can see what the machine returns.

9
00:00:42,974 --> 00:00:49,975
Run sends it through the whole pipeline: parse, layout, route,
translate, optimise, schedule, execute, measure.

10
00:00:49,975 --> 00:01:00,796
The replay is now driving every surface. Watch the step tick
off as the run completes.

11
00:01:00,796 --> 00:01:07,798
This is the same run, in the city. The geography IS the
pipeline: each district is one compiler stage.

12
00:01:07,798 --> 00:01:15,802
West to east along the boulevard: Program Port, IR Foundry,
Layout Exchange, Routing Transit, Translation Refinery,
Optimization Works, Scheduling Tower.

13
00:01:15,802 --> 00:01:22,813
Then the QPU Grid where it executes, the Measurement Harbor
where shots land, and the Classical Control Center for
feedback.

14
00:01:22,813 --> 00:01:36,773
Everything derives from the trace and the current tick, so we
can rewind and watch causality again.

15
00:01:36,773 --> 00:01:43,310
The convoy carries the compiled job. It arrives at each
district exactly when that stage emits its events.

16
00:01:43,310 --> 00:01:53,671
Now the idea this visualisation exists for: the circuit you
write is not the circuit that runs.

17
00:01:53,671 --> 00:02:02,191
This program needs qubits that sit far apart on the device.
The router must physically move them together.

18
00:02:02,191 --> 00:02:15,739
Each SWAP the compiler inserts exchanges two logical-qubit
banners between physical pylons, at the exact tick it happens.

19
00:02:15,739 --> 00:02:25,286
Logical qubit q0 is a name tag. The pylon underneath it is the
physical hardware. A SWAP changes which is which.

20
00:02:25,286 --> 00:02:32,289
Compare Mode puts the circuit you wrote next to the circuit
that actually ran, with the compiler metrics.

21
00:02:32,289 --> 00:02:40,818
Gate count, two-qubit gates, SWAPs inserted, depth. This is
where the cost of connectivity becomes concrete.

22
00:02:40,818 --> 00:02:53,877
Turn on the noise model and run again. Noise does not produce
a wrong answer: it changes the distribution.

23
00:02:53,877 --> 00:03:00,436
Accessible 2D Mode is the complete product with no WebGL at
all, for machines and users that need it.

24
00:03:00,436 --> 00:03:07,938
Every number carries its provenance: measured counts are
SAMPLED, compiler metrics are COMPUTED, probabilities are
EXACT.

25
00:03:07,938 --> 00:03:16,256
An Active Simplifications panel states what the model is NOT,
including that playback pacing is presentation time.

26
00:03:16,256 --> 00:03:22,966
You are not limited to looking down at it. Press four, and you
are standing in the street.

27
00:03:22,966 --> 00:03:30,769
Walking the Processing Boulevard: traffic, people, street
lamps, and the districts you just watched the job pass
through.

28
00:03:30,769 --> 00:03:39,421
The city is deliberately ordinary. It is scaffolding for the
pipeline, and the Legend says exactly which parts mean
anything.

29
00:03:39,421 --> 00:03:53,432
Day, golden hour, and night are presentation settings only.
They never touch the science underneath.

30
00:03:53,432 --> 00:03:59,433
The mission that carries the central idea: a program whose
qubits start far apart on the device.

31
00:03:59,433 --> 00:04:11,460
Run it with a deliberately poor initial layout and the router
must insert SWAP after SWAP just to bring qubits together.

32
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:23,302
Then the learner changes one setting — the layout method — and
runs the identical circuit again.

33
00:04:23,302 --> 00:04:33,115
Same circuit, same hardware, a different layout — and the
router has less distance to pay for. That is the compiler
decision made visible, and it is the point of the whole

34
00:04:33,115 --> 00:04:48,971
A guided tour narrates the same pipeline hands-free, sixteen
chapters, each stating how certain its claims are.

35
00:04:48,971 --> 00:04:55,974
The City Legend lists everything that moves, what it means,
what triggers it, and how certain it is.

36
00:04:55,974 --> 00:05:03,500
The rule that makes this teach rather than mislead: vehicles
carry jobs and measured bits. Never a quantum state.

37
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:10,001
No learning outcomes are claimed yet: the assessment
instrument and study protocol are published, unrun.

38
00:05:10,001 --> 00:05:15,003
QSimCity. Apache-2.0, original artwork, runs offline. Thank
you for watching.
